title: Writes and atomicity
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: One write is one transaction — rows, log, HEAD and every derived key space — and the map grows at the start of each write.

!! One write, one transaction

One `log::Writer` write is one write transaction of the store, and it holds
everything the write changes:

* the field rows;
* the log's entries and the before of each operation, and HEAD;
* ''every'' index (derived key space).

It commits entirely or not at all. So the indexes are consistent with HEAD by
construction: there is no snapshot-plus-replay scheme, and nothing on the read path
"catches up" to HEAD.

Bulk writers (reconcile, a large watcher flush) accumulate their index updates in
memory, a chunk at a time, and write each chunk once. There is one writer at a time:
the executor and the API already serialise writes.

!! A record decoded once, written once

Within one write transaction nothing else writes, so what the transaction holds in
memory is no cache: there is nothing to invalidate. A record ([[Store tables]]) is
read and decoded the first time the transaction needs it (`KvTxn::stored_record`);
every operation of the transaction — and every read it makes, through `Read`'s
`record` hook — works on that decoded form; each record changed is encoded and put
once, at the commit (`flush_records`, `commit.records` in the slow log). Before,
each operation read, decoded, re-encoded and wrote the whole record on its own: a
`set_field` decoded it three times and encoded it twice, and N writes to a record of
K rows cost N × K (`tests/kv_cost.rs` counts both, `KvStore::record_codings`).

The form is lent, never copied (`KvTxn::with_record`, `with_record_mut`), and it is
kept ''by field'': each field's values, in the order the fields come, as the encoding
already groups them. A write to a field changes that field in place — a value pushed,
a value or the field taken off —, and a read of one field (`Read::rows_named`, through
the `field` hook: `derive_row`, the `before` of a `set_field`) copies that field only;
only what needs the whole record (`KvTxn::record_rows`: a deletion, a restoration, a
read of the metarecord) copies it whole. A field emptied keeps its place, empty, and
one written again comes last, so the rows come back in the order they did when the
record was a list of rows. Decoding the record by field also allocates a field's name
once, not once per row. Before, a `set_field` copied the whole record about ten times
— four reads of it, each copying the decoded form and then its rows —, and 5000
writes to one record took 17 s; they copy the field now (`KvStore::rows_copied`,
`tests/kv_cost.rs`: the same count for a record of 50 fields and of 5000).

When entries of a write to many records name the record ([[Mass writes in the store]]), a field is read with them applied (`mass::effective_values`, which applies
only that field's entries: an entry writes one field), and a write first takes the
field as they leave it, then fixes what it wrote — applying the entries again on
reading gives the same values.

A read of the committed store (`Source::values`, the candidates of a scan) still
decodes the whole record to read one field: the encoding gives no field alone, the
values of a group having no length that lets a reader pass over them. Letting it would
be a change of the persisted format.

A walk of the table itself — the drop of what deleted metarecords left, the
re-filing of a renumbering, a layout conversion — writes out the decoded records
first and forgets them. No bound is set on what a transaction holds: a watcher batch
is at most 4096 events, a reconcile goes in batches, and a write to many records
reads no record ([[Mass writes in the store]]).

!! The map, and how large a write can be

LMDB reserves the whole ''map'' — the address range the file is mapped into — when
it opens, and can enlarge it only while the process has no transaction open. A write
transaction can therefore never write more than the room left in the map when it
started (the map's size minus what the store already uses). Too large a write fails
whole with `MDB_MAP_FULL`, without corruption.

So, just before each write transaction (`KvStore::grow_map`, `map_to_grant`), the
daemon gives it room:

* the room granted is the ''free space of the disk'', capped at 128 GiB per store;
* it is renewed only when it has fallen below what must be guaranteed — all of the free space on a disk with less than 128 GiB free (store and free space shrink together, so this is not a resize at every write), 64 GiB otherwise.

Consequences:

* a write of any size — the first reconcile of a large tree — commits, unless the disk is full or a single write exceeds 64 GiB;
* the address space holds the maps of some 900 loaded repositories (a process has 128 TiB of it);
* where the free space cannot be read, the room is at least what the store already holds.

Two earlier rules failed: a fixed 1 TiB map ran out of address space at about 120
repositories, and doubling the map when half full failed any write larger than the
store already was.
